Ubuntu Main Inclusion Review — preliminary assessment
=====================================================

Status: DRAFT.  Not yet filed against Ubuntu.

This file is the maintainer's working notes for an eventual Main
Inclusion Review (MIR) bug for duckdb.  It is committed to the
repository so the assessment can be iterated on between now and
filing, and so future reviewers can see the maintainer's own
reading of the requirements.

The MIR process is documented at
  https://canonical-ubuntu-project.readthedocs-hosted.com/MIR/main-inclusion-review/
(content also mirrored historically at github.com/canonical/ubuntu-mir
commit 0ca04235 -- the redirect-only README in main).

The MIR process applies only to packages destined for Ubuntu *main*.
This packaging targets a PPA in *universe* as the first step (see
debian/README.distribution); MIR comes later, only if there is a
specific consumer in main that needs libduckdb1.

Prerequisites not yet met
-------------------------

These must be true *before* a MIR bug can be filed, regardless of
whether the rest of the assessment is favourable:

1. The package must already be in Ubuntu universe.  Current
   distribution channel is GitHub Releases + (planned) Launchpad PPA;
   neither lands the package in universe automatically.  Path to
   universe: file an ITP with Debian, get sponsored upload to
   unstable, package syncs to Ubuntu the following devel cycle.
   This typically takes 3-12 months.

2. A consumer in main must request the dependency.  MIR rejects
   "users want it" alone; a specific main package or seed has to
   declare a Depends:/Recommends: on a libduckdb1 binary.  As of
   today no such consumer exists.

3. amd64-only is not sufficient.  MIR wants the package built for
   every architecture upstream supports.  DuckDB upstream supports
   amd64, arm64, and (with caveats) riscv64.  The packaging CI
   currently builds amd64 only -- arm64 is on the improvement list
   in this repo and would be required before submission.

Per-section assessment
----------------------

Each [Section] below corresponds to a section in the MIR reporter's
template.  Statements are OK unless flagged with **ISSUE** or
**TODO**.

[Availability]
- Builds and works on amd64.  Verified end-to-end on Debian
  trixie/sid and Ubuntu jammy/noble via the package CI matrix.
- **TODO**: extend matrix to arm64 (native GH-Actions runners are
  now available) before MIR submission.

[Rationale]
- DuckDB is a widely-used in-process analytical SQL database
  (the "SQLite of OLAP").  Heavy use in Python/R analytics
  pipelines, increasing presence in data-engineering and
  embedded-analytics use cases.
- **ISSUE**: MIR wants a *specific* main consumer that requires
  libduckdb1.  Without one, the recommendation is usually
  "leave it in universe".  Possible consumer cases that would
  strengthen the rationale: a Python data-science meta-package
  in main pulling in python3-duckdb; an analytics CLI in main
  linking against libduckdb1.  None exists today.

[Security]
- No suid/sgid binaries.
- No system services, timers, or recurring jobs (in-process
  library + CLI).
- No privileged port exposure (in-process; no network listener).
- CVE history (from github.com/duckdb/duckdb security advisories):
    GHSA-vmp8-hg63-v2hp  medium  Encryption Crypto implementation
    GHSA-w2gf-jxc9-pf2q  medium  sniff_csv path-access bypass
    GHSA-7q92-pph9-5686  medium  GH-Actions expression injection
                                 (build infra, not runtime)
  All medium severity; all fixed upstream; no high/critical.
- **ISSUE (vendoring)**: DuckDB vendors eleven third-party
  libraries in third_party/: fastpforlib, fmt, fsst,
  hyperloglog, mbedtls, miniz, re2, skiplistlib, utf8proc,
  yyjson, zstd.  Of these, mbedtls (crypto), zstd
  (decompression), and re2 (regex) have non-trivial CVE
  histories.  Per MIR vendoring rules, the owning team must
  commit to upstream-sync-on-CVE for the package's lifetime
  (release + ESM).  See "Vendored code refresh process"
  below for how this would be handled.

[Quality assurance - function/usage]
- `apt install duckdb` produces an immediately usable CLI;
  `duckdb` opens a REPL without configuration.
- libduckdb-dev ships a worked C-API example (demo_capi) plus
  CMake config; "hello world" linking against the library
  fits in five lines (see debian/tests/c-link.c).

[Quality assurance - maintenance]
- Upstream is healthy (DuckDB Labs / DuckDB Foundation; regular
  releases on a stable cadence; large GitHub community).
- Upstream bug tracker: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues
- **ISSUE (owning team)**: this packaging is currently
  one-person (the Maintainer in debian/control).  MIR requires
  a team-level commitment with management backing for the
  lifetime of an Ubuntu release (5 years standard, 10 with ESM).
  Path forward: either get a Canonical or community team to
  formally subscribe before MIR submission, or accept that MIR
  will land in "Incomplete" pending that subscription.

[Quality assurance - testing]
- The packaging disables upstream unittests at build
  (BUILD_UNITTESTS=OFF in debian/rules) for build-budget
  reasons (see the DISABLE_UNITY comment block in
  debian/rules); the full upstream test suite runs separately
  and is the upstream project's responsibility.
- Package autopkgtest suite covers seven scenarios:
    cli-smoke         duckdb --version / one-shot query / exit codes
    csv               COPY FROM/TO CSV + byte-level diff
    two-process-rw    process A writes, process B reads back
    c-link            compile against -dev + INSERT/SELECT
    cxx-link          C++ API: DuckDB / Connection / Query
    arrow             duckdb_query_arrow column/row count
    multithread       4 threads x 1000 inserts -> 4000 rows
- All seven pass on Debian trixie locally; CI runs them
  against jammy/noble/sid/trixie automatically on every push.

[Dependencies]
- All Build-Depends are already in main: cmake, ninja-build,
  g++, pandoc, pkgconf, python3, git, debhelper.  Verified by
  the successful CI build on jammy (which is the strictest of
  our matrix legs for main vs universe distinction).
- Runtime: libc6, libstdc++6, libgcc-s1.  All in main.

[Standards compliance]
- Standards-Version: 4.7.4 (latest as of 2026-03; bumped from
  4.7.0 with Priority: stripped from the source stanza per
  Policy 5.6.6 since 4.7.3).
- FHS-compliant: /usr/bin, /usr/lib/$DEB_HOST_MULTIARCH,
  /usr/include, /usr/share/doc.
- Git-Tag-Tagger and Git-Tag-Info fields (Policy 5.6.32-33,
  4.7.3) deliberately not added: these are reserved for the
  tag2upload service, which this packaging does not use.

[Maintenance/Owner]
- See [QA - maintenance] ISSUE above.

[Static linking] / [Vendored code]
- The shared library libduckdb.so statically embeds the in-tree
  extensions listed in debian/rules' DUCKDB_EXTENSIONS variable
  (autocomplete, core_functions, icu, json, parquet).  These are
  DuckDB's own code, not third-party vendoring.
- The third_party/ tree (eleven libraries listed under [Security])
  IS third-party vendored code.  Per MIR vendoring rules:
    - Maintainer commits to refreshing affected vendored copies
      when upstream-of-DuckDB issues a security fix.
    - Refresh process documented in debian/README.source
      (TODO: presently README.source does not yet document the
      vendor-refresh procedure; this gap must be closed before
      MIR submission).
- This package is not Rust-based.  No Cargo.lock.

[Background information]
- Upstream Name: duckdb
- Upstream URL: https://duckdb.org/
- Upstream code: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb
- Packaging fork: https://github.com/mgajda/duckdb-deb
- The package descriptions in debian/control explain the purpose
  of each binary package clearly.

Vendored code refresh process (TODO)
------------------------------------

When upstream-of-DuckDB issues a security fix for a vendored
library (e.g. mbedtls fixes CVE-YYYY-NNNN), the response is:

1. Wait for the next DuckDB upstream release that incorporates
   the fix.  DuckDB upstream syncs third_party/ on its own
   cadence; the fix may be available within days or may take a
   point release.
2. If a release is not imminent and the CVE is high-severity,
   apply a backport patch under debian/patches/ that updates
   only the affected file(s) in third_party/<lib>/, with a
   `Origin: backport, <upstream-url>` header per DEP-3.
3. Rebuild and re-test (autopkgtest must still pass).
4. Upload through the same channel as a normal release.

This process is NOT yet implemented; the gap is acknowledged.
Pre-MIR work items:
  - Add an `uscan` watch on each third_party/<lib> if feasible
  - Document the exact backport-patch creation steps in
    debian/README.source

De-vendoring assessment (committed snapshot — 2026-05-19)
---------------------------------------------------------

Inventory of third_party/ entries that are actually compiled into
the shipped binaries (the rest are header-only or for unenabled
extensions and don't matter):

| Library      | Linked into          | Debian package      | De-vendoring effort |
|--------------|----------------------|---------------------|---------------------|
| mbedtls      | libduckdb.so         | libmbedtls-dev      | HARD -- DuckDB wrapper opens private struct fields via #define MBEDTLS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ACCESS; needs API rewrite |
| zstd         | libduckdb.so         | libzstd-dev         | Easy -- public API only |
| re2          | libduckdb.so         | libre2-dev          | Easy -- public API only, but ABI churn risk between re2 versions |
| fmt          | libduckdb.so         | libfmt-dev          | Easy but risky -- fmt's ABI changes between minor versions; DuckDB pins a specific vendored copy for reproducibility |
| utf8proc     | libduckdb.so         | libutf8proc-dev     | Easy -- stable public API |
| yyjson       | libduckdb.so         | libyyjson-dev       | Easy -- public API only |
| miniz        | libduckdb.so         | (libminizip-ng-dev) | DIFFERENT API -- minizip-ng is a fork with breaking changes; stays vendored |
| brotli       | parquet extension    | libbrotli-dev       | Easy -- public API |
| lz4          | parquet extension    | liblz4-dev          | Easy -- public API |
| snappy       | parquet extension    | libsnappy-dev       | Easy -- public API, but DuckDB inlines .cc into the parquet extension target |
| thrift       | parquet extension    | libthrift-dev       | Easy -- public API |
| (parquet)    | parquet extension    | (libparquet-dev)    | DIFFERENT API -- Apache Arrow's Parquet C++; DuckDB ships its own writer; stays vendored |

DuckDB's upstream CMake has no `WITH_SYSTEM_*` knobs; every
de-vendoring choice requires a local patch under debian/patches/
that modifies third_party/<lib>/CMakeLists.txt to skip the
add_subdirectory() and instead find_package() the system version,
plus a tweak of the link-targets to refer to the imported target.

Priority order if we proceed:

1. **zstd, brotli, lz4, snappy, thrift** -- low-risk de-vendoring
   wins, all are well-trodden Debian packages with stable ABIs,
   all are CVE-relevant compressor/codec libraries.
2. **utf8proc, yyjson** -- stable, public API; low CVE risk but
   easy de-vendoring.
3. **re2, fmt** -- public API but ABI churn risk; needs careful
   per-distro testing in CI.
4. **mbedtls** -- requires upstream wrapper rewrite to drop the
   MBEDTLS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ACCESS escape hatch.  Until then, stays
   vendored with backport-on-CVE.

Estimated effort: ~1 day per library for steps 1+2 (CMake patch +
header-include sweep + CI re-test); ~1 week each for steps 3-4
(ABI testing + wrapper rewrite).

Decision: defer the de-vendoring implementation until the MIR
process surfaces a specific blocker.  The eleven-library vendoring
burden is acknowledged in the [Static linking] section above and
will be the maintainer's commitment for the package lifetime if
MIR is filed without de-vendoring.
